{{ElectionsNY}}
The '''2022 United States House of Representatives elections in New York''' were held on November 8, 2022, to elect the 26 [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representatives]] from the [[New York (state)|State of New York]], one from each of the state's 26 [[New York's congressional districts|congressional districts]]. The elections coincided with elections for [[2022 New York gubernatorial election|governor]], [[2022 United States Senate election in New York|U.S. Senate]], [[2022 New York Attorney General election|attorney general]], [[2022 New York State Comptroller election|comptroller]], [[2022 New York State Senate election|state senate]], and [[2022 New York State Assembly election|assembly]], and various other [[2022 United States elections#State elections|state]] and [[2022 United States elections#Local elections|local elections]].

Following the [[2020 United States census|2020 census]], New York lost one seat in the U.S. House. Incumbent representatives [[Lee Zeldin]] ([[Republican Party (United States)|R]]), [[Thomas Suozzi]] ([[Democratic Party (United States)|D]]), [[Kathleen Rice]] (D), [[John Katko]] (R), [[Christopher Jacobs (politician)|Chris Jacobs]] (R), and [[Joe Sempolinski]] (R) retired. Representatives [[Carolyn Maloney]] (D) and [[Mondaire Jones]] (D) lost renomination in their primary contests. The primary elections were set to happen on June 28, but due to a court-ordered redraw of the state's Congressional maps, they were held on August 23.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|date=April 29, 2022 |title=N.Y. moves some primaries to August after a judge tosses maps |language=en |work=NPR |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/04/29/1095657939/new-york-voting-august |access-date=May 10, 2022}}</ref> Due in part to [[Kathy Hochul]]'s relatively weak performance in the [[2022 New York gubernatorial election|governor's race]] and heavy pro-Republican turnout, the Democratic Party lost four seats.

With 11 seats held, this is the most seats won by Republicans since [[2000 United States House of Representatives elections in New York|2000]], and the best performance in the popular vote since [[2002 United States House of Representatives elections in New York|2002]].
{{Horizontal TOC|nonum=yes|limit=2}}

==2020–22 redistricting controversy==
Following the [[2020 United States census|2020 census]], New York lost one Congressional seat and its Independent Redistricting Commission (IRC) attempted to draw a new map. However they could not reach an agreement on the map, and the Democratic-dominated [[Legislature|New York State Legislature]] drew their own new Congressional map. In April 2022, in the lead-up to the [[2022 midterms]], the [[New York State Court of Appeals]] struck down the map, known infamously as the "Hochulmander" (named after [[New York State Governor]] [[Kathy Hochul]]) as an unconstitutional partisan [[gerrymander]] that failed to follow the proper procedures.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://nypost.com/2022/04/27/court-of-appeals-axes-new-maps-for-congress-ny-senate/|title=Court of Appeals axes new maps for Congress, NY Senate|date=April 27, 2022}}</ref> The court then assigned an Independent Special Master to create a new map. In the [[2022 United States House of Representatives elections|2022 House election]] in New York, under the new map, the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] flipped three seats, bringing the new seat count of 15 for the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] and 11 held by Republicans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.politico.com/2022-election/results/new-york/house/|title=New York House Election Results 2022: Live Map &#124; Midterm Races by District|website=www.politico.com}}</ref> The court-drawn map is often credited with helping the Republican Party win back control of the House, due to the narrow margin of the Republican majority.<ref>[https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-york-democrats-help-republicans-to-a-house-majority-maps-gerrymandering-misha-tseytlin-independent-commission-court-11669412408 New York Democrats Help GOP to a Congressional Majority] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231119184358/https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-york-democrats-help-republicans-to-a-house-majority-maps-gerrymandering-misha-tseytlin-independent-commission-court-11669412408 |date=November 19, 2023 }}, Wall Street Journal, November 25, 2022</ref>

==Overview==
In some races, candidates appeared on multiple ballot lines, with Democratic candidates often appearing on the Working Families line and Republican candidates often appearing on the Conservative line. However, they all caucus with either the Democrats or the Republicans.
